 merry way  39 of the patients never returned to the  clinic for their scheduled follow-ups  and let's be real treat me like that I  probably would not have either and from  there we had 12 patients who lost more  than 20 pounds and just six of which who  maintained it a year later and if we  round that off nicely we have our sad  little 95 of diets fail stat now this  paper also reviewed some previous  studies that also tend to support the  suggestion that 95 of weight loss  interventions failed but these were all  pre-1959 where the approach was mainly  very low calorie diets prescribed with  very little guidance both during and  following the intervention in other  words this was a prime example of the  old adage they didn't fail at the diet  the diet failed them looking at this I  feel like it's comical that we expected  any of this to work even the study's  primary author himself was quoted in the  New York Times in 99 saying that was the  state of the art in 1959 I being sort of  surprised that people are still citing  it I know we do better these days I'll  say you know I am certainly not a fan of  most diets out there but it would be  unprofessional of me to subscribe to  this statistic today knowing that the  diet these poor participants were  expected to succeed at was truly the  pits so yeah let's agree that this study  isn't the best piece of research ever  done it's a miracle that they had as  much retention as they did and as the  author said these days we do better but  how much better are the results well a  lot of modern diets throw around their  own success statistics most of which  seem a lot more promising than that  original 95 of diets fail the challenge  is that a lot of privately funded  research for commercial diets tends not  to study or at least not report  long-term stats and that's likely  because if they did their numbers would  look far less enticing Noom for example  cites a success rate of 77 percent of  their participants being able to lose  weight with the app but after a year  they actually lost half of their study  participants and their data actually  shows that only one percent of people  who use NuMe were still using it after  six months and poor adherence and  follow-up is a general problem in diet  research with most studies looking at  diet and weight loss retention only up  to an average of 16 months most people  want to maintain their lower weight for  decades not months but still this is  pretty much the best that we got so what  does the more recent and arguably better  research in this area suggests is your  true chances of weight loss success well  the first question that we really need  to answer is what exactly do we mean by  success is just losing some amount of  weight a success what about weight loss  that doesn't improve our health is that  considered a success because Let Me Tell  You Folks most weight loss diets are  looking strictly at the number on the  scale so trying to answer the question  How likely are you to succeed on your  diet is really not a straightforward  question but to me weight loss is not  useful if it can't be maintained so to  get the best sense of longevity we need  to speak to the eight-year look ahead  trial unlike the diet from the 1950  study that we discussed the look-ahead  trials approach was pretty well  integrated multi-faceted evidence-based  and supportive it included one-on-one  support with health professionals  including dietitians nutrition  interventions that were tailored to the  individual a focus on developing  positive balanced behaviors  incorporation of other key weight loss  factors beyond diet like sleep and  exercise and most importantly continued  professional support after the program  for weight maintenance I would say this  is a pretty good template of what to  look for in a supportive weight  management program now we know from  other data that having long-term support  and follow-up is the key to improving  chances of 