etabolism slows down it happens most  studies estimate a five percent decline  which could mean needing let's say 50 to  150 fewer calories per day just to  maintain your new weight number two we  subconsciously just move less most of  this is in neat or non-exercise activity  thermogenesis so not necessarily less  time in the gym but rather things that  you don't even notice that you do so  research on this area suggests that  dieters burn about 35 percent or 350  calories less every day just by  subconsciously walking standing and  fidgeting less compared to similar folks  who have never lost weight so even  though the world is like really obsessed  with metabolism boosting and declines  when it comes to Weight Management this  unconscious movement actually plays a  much bigger role and that brings me to  number three hunger folks ghrelin is a  [ __ ]  research consistently shows that our  hunger hormones like graylin become  elevated Beyond Baseline in folks who  have lost weight so this is not just low  willpower but physiologically when your  body shrinks your appetite legitimately  grows and this isn't even speaking to  the psychological piece we know that  when you feel psychological deprivation  like denying yourself foods that you  really really love and crave you're more  likely to overeat them when you get the  rare chance you know it's the classic  last summer mentality or cheat day  situation that I talk about all the time  motivation also tends to wane over time  for obvious and understandable reasons  since fighting against all of these  normal healthy bodily processes gets  exhausting as again this is not just a  matter of poor willpower or your body  feeling you it's literally just trying  to keep you alive  ah ah staying alive staying alive  staying alive staying alive so yeah I'm  not gonna sugarcoat it permanent weight  loss is very very hard and it will be  disproportionately hard and possibly  completely improbable for some people  due to genetics demographics and  environmental factors like socioeconomic  status access and support as we've  discussed it's not impossible across the  board and it's likely possible for more  than five percent of the population as  we previously thought what I think is a  lot more useful than knowing your  specific chance of losing weight is  knowing the best way that you should try  to do it and we do have research to give  us some insight into what successful  dieters do so if we look at National  weight control Registries we can see  data from more than 10 000 people who  have lost more than 10 percent of their  body weight and kept it off most of  which is for more than 10 years that's  pretty impressive data I would say now  among the top reported behaviors that  have resulted in long-term weight loss  weren't keto or intermittent fasting  they were things like regular exercise  increased vegetable consumption ensuring  nutritious food options are easily  available to them like for example  keeping veggies and fruit in the house  for like a fast snack and maintaining  conscious awareness of their intake so  for example knowing how to read and  Compare food labels at the store and a  lot of the little things that dieters do  to lose weight will need to be  maintained in order to keep it off the  more weight that you lose the more you  need to keep doing because if you lost  weight doing keto and intermittent  fasting for example and then you start  eating carbs plus breakfast again you  are very likely going to gain that  weight right back thanks to all of those  physiological adaptations that we just  discussed we also have some insight into  to the psychological components of a  successful diet for weight loss and  weight loss maintenance mainly that a  flexible approach to eating is more  successful than a rigid one that cuts  out certain bad foods or signs other  obsessive rules as you all know if  you're a regular here at Abby's kitchen  that means that no food should be off  limits and each food has its place in  your diet depending on the context and  timing so for example a birthday party  might mean a slice of birthday cake  while not having it become like a  fixture of your daily evening routine  research also confirms that having an  internal locus of control and strong  self-efficacy where you feel in control  and confident in your way of eating can  also help to increase your chances of  long-term success essentially the more  confident you feel in your understanding  of what you need to do to maintain your  weight loss the better it can be a  complex learning process that goes far  beyond just like maintain a certain  calorie intake so this is just yet  another reason why we encourage working  with an experienced Rd bottom line folks  weight loss is hard and weight loss  maintenance is really really hard if  you've died down to 150 pounds you are  always going to have to put in more  effort to maintain that weight than  someone who was always 150 pounds  especially if you're at the bottom end  of your body's preferred weight range of  200. weight loss maintenance is also  going to be harder for some folks than  others thanks to the Natural  physiological and psychological  mechanisms that we just discussed along  with a slew of other genetic and  environmental barriers so please folks  give yourself some Grace if you're  struggling to lose weight and maintain  it this is never going to be just a  matter of a calorie deficit and a lot of  willpower this is not your body failing  you or that you need to try harder next  time so let's try to just appreciate  that our body is doing what it's meant  to do even if it's maybe not what we  want it to do so for more tips on some  gentle flexible suggestions for losing  weight you can check out my video right  here and again a big thank you to Eric  for his expertise on this topic  definitely check out his Link in the  description and give him a follow at  unlocked Fitness Nutrition if you're  looking for some 101 support and on that  note that's all that I have for you guys  today if you like this video be sure to  give it the thumbs up leave me a comment  below with any questions that you might  have subscribe to the channel and I will  see you next time on Abby's kitchen bye  foreign  [Music]

